The AmigaOS layer of the owner's terminal programs on Zenix (roadmap block 3; DESIGN D27 section 9; Sumi, Maki and
Markdown, ports/sumi, ports/maki, ports/markdown; docs/d27-c-library.md, "Sumi on Zenix" and "Maki and Markdown on
Zenix")

What it is: the owner's programs call AmigaOS directly (they have no OS layer of their own), so their upstream files
compile unchanged over AmigaOS's names given here over the C library (GPL-2.0-only). One layer for the three, never a
copy each: dos.c is compiled once per program (build/PROG/zenix-dos.o) with the settings names that program maps
(ZENIX_TT_NAMES, the Makefile's TT_NAMES_PROG), and linked with the program's files, the text-UI library's pure build
(build/libtui-pure.a, ports/tui) and the C library's (build/libc-pure.a).

include/   amigados.h: the types, the structures upstream reads (FileInfoBlock, DateStamp, DateTime, FileHandle,
           DosPacket, the clipboard's request) and the calls, each one's meaning on Zenix in its head; the exec/, dos/,
           devices/, intuition/ and proto/ headers upstream includes are amigados.h (and so are those the text-UI
           library's headers include, in this build); proto/utf8.h (Maki's composing, never reached) and
           inline/macros.h (Markdown's call into openurl.library, never reached): no library opens on Zenix
dos.c      the calls: Open/Read/Write/Seek/Close/FGets (a handle is the descriptor + 1, so 0 stays "failed"; "*" the
           terminal, /dev/tty), Input/Output/IsInteractive, Lock/UnLock/Examine/ExNext/SameLock/CreateDir (a lock is
           the path, its device and inode), DeleteFile, Rename, SetProtection (the Unix mode, and root's owner),
           IoErr and Fault (errno and strerror), CompareDates, DateToStr, GetVar (getenv), Delay, SystemTags
           (system(), with SYS_Input and SYS_Output as the command's descriptors 0 and 1), FindTask, DOS packets
           (SendPkt/GetMsg: a pipe read when it has data), the reply ports' signal bits for the text-UI library's
           wait (tui_zenix_sigfd), the clipboard device (never opens), OpenLibrary (never opens)

Decided here (conservatively; for the main session to confirm):
- The names: only the program's own literals are mapped, nothing else is ever rewritten (a file the user names is a
  Unix path as typed): "ENVARC:NAME" is $HOME/.config/NAME for the program's names (Sumi; Maki and FileColours;
  Markdown: TT_NAMES_PROG), its folder made at the first start, mode 700 (the places file lists the files read or
  edited); "ENV:NAME" is no file (AmigaOS keeps a RAM copy of the settings there; on Zenix one place is enough, so that
  copy is never written and never read); "T:PROG-" is /tmp/PROG- (a file never named, kept when the terminal goes
  away). No HOME: no settings are kept.
- Protection bits: FileInfoBlock's protection carries the Unix mode (bits 8-19, bit 31 marking it) and AmigaOS's r, w
  and e bits, set when this user may not read or write the file, or it has no x bit (upstream then shows [read-only]
  and refuses to save over it; Maki's listing shows rwed as AmigaOS's List does, and its colours take a file with x
  and no name ending for a program). FIBF_DELETE is never set: a directory that cannot be written fails the save with
  its own message. SetProtection puts the mode back after a save (a new copy renamed over the old file) and, when root
  saves, the owner and group Examine saw. Comments: Zenix files have none; Examine gives an empty one. A soft link is
  listed as what it points to (stat, not lstat: upstream would take ST_SOFTLINK's 3, above 0, for a directory).
- Dates: DateStamp counts days from 1970 (AmigaOS: 1978); only this layer reads and writes it (CompareDates,
  DateToStr in local time), so upstream never sees the difference.
- "*" (AmigaOS's console of the program) is /dev/tty: Maki reading a pipe takes its keys from the terminal.
- Packets: upstream's ACTION_READ (Maki reading a pipe while it shows it) is a read done when the descriptor has data:
  SendPkt notes it on the reply port, GetMsg polls the descriptor (never waiting) and reads; the reply port's signal
  bit (16-31, as AmigaOS gives a program's own) names that descriptor to TuiWait (tui_zenix_sigfd, ports/tui), so the
  wait ends when the pipe has more. Other packets fail (DoPkt: ACTION_DISK_INFO asks the console whether it is CTerm;
  Zenix's is not). A read still out when Maki closes the pipe stays (upstream's PA_IGNORE): nothing writes into it.
- Ctrl-C: SetSignal never has it (in raw mode ^C is a key, which upstream takes as Esc in a prompt); a long search
  in Maki cannot be stopped by it.
- SystemTags: through sh (system()), in the terminal; upstream's default editor "Sumi" (Maki's v without $EDITOR) is
  run as Zenix's sumi.
- The stack: upstream swaps to a 32 KB stack of its own when the Shell gave less; on Zenix the program's stack is the
  loader's (D27 9 rule 5) and FindTask answers a task with enough, so upstream never swaps (each program's README has
  its measurement).

Tests: each program's (tools/sumi-test.sh, tools/maki-test.sh, tools/markdown-test.sh, in make check); their logs are
read by tools/screenlog.py.

If upstream starts to use an AmigaOS call amigados.h does not have, the build says so (an undeclared function); the
call is added here, once, for every program.
